IMPORT prelude

-- Misrepresentation Act 1967 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation
-- (plain-text rendering of the Singapore Statutes Online text; not the official text).
-- LAYER 1, THE NOUNS: declarations only, no rules.  Written from the text alone.
--
-- The Act defines no term, so almost every fact it turns on ("a misrepresentation",
-- "entitled to rescind", "fraudulently", "loss", "equitable") is a plain true-or-false input
-- on the function that needs it.  The only nouns the rules share are the three lists of
-- alternatives below.  Names here must not collide with those of the copied Interpretation Act
-- module (sgia-p1-definitions.l4), which defines Definition, `Kind of definition`, Means,
-- Includes and others.

§ `Nouns of the Misrepresentation Act 1967`

-- s 2(2) says "the court or arbitrator may declare the contract subsisting and award damages
-- in lieu of rescission".  Those are the two bodies the text names; anything else is not named.
DECLARE `Who is deciding` IS ONE OF
    `The court`
    `An arbitrator`
    `Another body`

-- The step s 2(2) lets the court or arbitrator take.
DECLARE `Step under section 2(2)` IS ONE OF
    `declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission`

-- Ss 1 to 3 speak of "a person" and "a party".  The Interpretation Act 1965 s 2(1) says what
-- those words include (module ma-reading); this list is what a person can say about themselves.
DECLARE `Kind of party` IS ONE OF
    `An individual`
    `A company`
    `An association`
    `Another body of persons`
    `Something else`

-- Section 2(2) speaks of "the court".  The Interpretation Act 1965 s 2(1) says what "court"
-- means (module ma-reading); this list is what a person can say about the body in front of them.
DECLARE `Body asked about` IS ONE OF
    `A court of competent jurisdiction in Singapore`
    `Any other court`
    `Any other body`
